{
    # Power on/off priorities
    #     A value 1 - 100 (other values will cause the process to terminate with an error.)
    #       1 = Power on/off first
    #      50 = Assigned to devices which have not been explicitly assigned a power on/off priority (VMs).  If dependents are listed appropriately then automatic priority assignment should suffice.
    #     100 = Power on/off last
    #     If a device does not specify a power on/off priority, it will be assigned priority 50.
    #     Parent/Dependent relationships will always be honored over manual priorities.
    #
    # Dependents
    #     Device "A" is considered a parent of device "B" for ALL DEVICES where device "B" appears as a dependent of device "A"
    #         Any single device may have any number of parents.
    #         A parent device may be a dependent of another device.
    #         A device MAY NOT be a dependent (in any fashion --- parent, grandparent, great-grandparent, etc) of any of its own dependents.  This will cause the process to terminate with an error.
    #         There must be at least 1 device per site which has no parents.  These are referred to as "root devices".  There may be multiple root devices.
    #     Dependents will be powered off prior to powering off any of its parents.
    #     All parents of a device must be powered on prior to any of its dependents being powered on.
    #
    # If a device is type "esxiHost", then all virtual machines running on the host are automatic dependents of the host and given a shutdown priority of 50.
    #     To control the order of start up and/or shutdown, assign priorities to specific VMs.
    #
    # Command examples:
    # > PowerOn-Site -Config <System.String> [-Sites <System.String[]>] [[-Devices <System.String[]>] | [-ExcludeDevices <System.String[]>]] [-ListOnly|-ListAll] [<CommonParameters>]
    # > PowerOff-Site -Config <System.String> [-Sites <System.String[]>] [-Devices <System.String[]>] [<CommonParameters>]
    #
    #    Config         - Path to JSON file containing deviceTypes, sites configuration data.
    #    Sites          - A list of site names defined in Config which will be powered on or off.
    #    Devices        - A list of devices defined in Config which will be powered on or off.
    #    ExcludeDevices - A list of devices defined in Config which will NOT be powered on or off.
    #    ListOnly       - List
    #
    # Devices and ExcludeDevices are mutually exclusive.
    # If Sites and Devices are

    #
    # If both
    #
    # The following common parameters are supported and passed on to the power on/off scripts.  The scripts are expected to honor these and act accordingly.
    #    WhatIf  - If specified, no action will be taken.
    #    Confirm - If specified, each action will require the user to confirm the action before the action is taken.
    #    Verbose - If specified, detailed information is output to the console.
    #
    # Device Types:
    #     Any number of device types can be created.
    #         typeName       = A label used to associate a pair of scripts used to start up and shutdown devices labelled as "typeName"
    #         startupScript  = Path to a Powershell script used to power on a device.
    #         shutdownScript = Path to a Powershell script used to power off a device.
    #         getStateScript = Path to a Powershell script used to determine the state of a device.
    #             Possible return values:
    #                 POWERED_ON: The device is powered on and ready.
    #                 POWERED_OFF: The device is powered off.
    #             getStateScript may wish to pause until it can definitively respond.
    #
    #     If a deviceType is missing one of the scripts, the process will terminate with an error.
    #         If you do not have a specific script to use for a device type, then use StartStop_Skeleton.ps1.  This script will include all parameters required for this process to function.  Further
    #         it can be used as the basis for your own customized scripts.
    #     If a script cannot be located when this process starts, it will terminate with an error.
        "deviceTypes": [
             {
                "typeName": "router",
                "startupScript": "path_to_startup.ps1",
                "shutdownScript": "path_to_shutdown.ps1"
            },
